#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "001af492-25b1-5f75-b030-668708eee118" :question/math-academy-id "e-15602" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/assets/a664a3af1ba466491d209dadd32a3be026a5179ac2d0281b55f30917f9ab016b.png)\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3699391-68e8-5c59-8ca2-83fded9f22de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Parallel To a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "7a6038c2-3f7b-5dfa-a9e2-256b246fcc20" :question/math-academy-id "e-15603" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/assets/a4c6f7b2422f1515e1708bf1641dcbec57eeb2d85e4ac03bd98668db34088afc.png)\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5-t \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the line passes through the point $(5,3,1)$ and is parallel to the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/assets/11701a2d63851a3f241d8bd905693abaaa6eda6fe4ba0c5710c92d8189595ffd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "debd4ccc-de40-55a6-9236-9bf9fff0bdb1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "173b99e8-d00c-57c8-995e-ba198361480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15604" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/assets/53386f04f4025fda6ab579113a8ecb53a19f4dc0a77903adc05bac3a71429717.png)" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(3,3,-5)$ and $Q(1,0,-1)$. Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -3t \\\\ 4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2t \\\\ 3-3t \\\\ -5+4t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-2t \\\\ y=3-3t \\\\ z=-5+4t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d275a218-aa12-54bd-a4ff-4be892226f4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "a9c13555-9d30-5dfc-be5a-13f0597c2ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-15605" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(1,-5,3)$ and $Q(0, 4, -4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 9t \\\\ -7t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-t \\\\ -5+9t \\\\ 3-7t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t \\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ are the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/assets/63d8bf337e8d7fa2323eb049b51d40349564336a12d53ffd3259b1e7211ef7dd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6871d1aa-9f43-51f3-ad0c-cddeed1f838e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314726} {:answer/id #uuid "b1495c16-40fd-5785-b0ea-1e151caa533a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314727} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4e5eee-575c-5ec4-b3b3-3b5045b4c69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314728} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4e5d5d-1c8d-5f7e-a978-461e3024711d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314729} {:answer/id #uuid "7cca786d-0715-5171-a8cd-8085bf3d5315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cca786d-0715-5171-a8cd-8085bf3d5315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314730} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9248b6-66a9-5d2e-8fe8-8128719ac3dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5a90c4d-c74b-5ee6-a22e-f77049796bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-126996" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{4}-2t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}-2t \\\\ 3+t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -2t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-p